ObjectBrowserPage: await the locator assertions.

expect(locator).toHaveCount() returns a Promise. Two call sites dropped it, so
the assertion polled in the background while the test kept running.

checkSingleBreadcrumb() is the damaging one. In "Folder double-click
disallowed" the sequence is:

    await objectBrowserPage.doubleClickFolder(folderName);
    await objectBrowserPage.checkSingleBreadcrumb(folderName);   // not awaited
    await objectBrowserPage.clickBreadcrumb(1);                  // runs immediately

The double-click routes into the folder asynchronously (onFileClick ->
withLoading -> router.push, then a list call over the network). Because the
assertion was not awaited, clickBreadcrumb(1) fired concurrently and pushed the
route back to the bucket root, so the 'testdata' crumb never settled. The
background poll then reported, after the test body had moved on:

    Expected: 1
    Received: 0
    4 x locator resolved to 0 elements

Timing-dependent, which is why one describe block went flaky and the other
failed through all retries.

expectItems() had the same unawaited toHaveCount, which silently weakened the
row-count check.

